  "articleSummary": "The article argues that AI-enabled cyber threats grow through two compounding capabilities—longer autonomous agent operation and broader orchestration of sub-agents—rather than a single spectacular breakthrough. It cites METR’s task-duration research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and “LLMjacking,” where stolen cloud credentials let attackers run LLM workloads at victims’ expense. Its central warning is that cheap, repeated attempts can turn low individual success rates into substantial aggregate risk while overwhelming human-scaled defenses and undermining pricing-based access controls. A pointed, darkly humorous security analysis aimed at skeptical engineering and security teams, it repeatedly uses Terminator/Skynet imagery to frame endurance, distributed coordination, and relentless billing as the real threat.",
